    {
      "id": "rc_79WJzH2aJnN7SVuCNe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 185,
      "y": 2905,
      "width": 571,
      "height": 2825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ay",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1340401278,
      "version": 342,
      "versionNonce": 358196734,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786262114205,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "package _1inheritanceEx01;\n\npublic class Usemanager {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Manager m=new Manager();\n        m.setEmp(\"Ashish\",50000);\n        m.setBonus(50000);\n        System.out.println(\"Mannager name : \"+m.getName());\n        System.out.println(\"Income \" +m.getIncome());\n    }\n}\n\n\npackage _1inheritanceEx01;\n\nclass Manager extends Emp {\n    private double bonus;\n\n    public void setBonus(double bonus) {\n        this.bonus = bonus;\n    }\n\n    public double getBonus() {\n        return bonus;\n    }\n\n    public double getIncome() {\n        double total;\n        total = getSal() + bonus;\n        return total;\n    }\n\n}\n\n\npackage _1inheritanceEx01;\n\npublic class Emp {\n    private String name;\n    private double sal;\n\n    public void setEmp(String name, double sal) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.sal = sal;\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    public double getSal() {\n        return sal;\n    }\n\n}\n\n\nthis Keyword\n============\n\n1. `this` is a reference that refers to the current object.\n\n2. Inside an instance method, if we access an instance\n   member directly without an object reference, we can\n   understand it as `this.member`.\n\nExample:\n---------\npublic String getName() {\n    return name;\n}\n\nFor understanding:\n-------------------\nreturn name;\n      ↓\nreturn this.name;\n\n\n3. Similarly:\n\ngetSal();\n   ↓\nthis.getSal();\n\n\n4. When instance variable and method parameter have the\n   same name, we use `this` to differentiate them.\n\nExample:\n---------\npublic void setEmp(String name, double sal) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.sal = sal;\n}\n\nHere:\n--> this.name → instance variable\n--> name      → method parameter\n\n--> this.sal  → instance variable\n--> sal       → method parameter\n\n\n5. Important:\n   `name` does NOT need to be literally written as\n   `this.name` in the source code.\n\n   We use `this.name` mainly to understand that `name`\n   belongs to the current object.\n\n6. Simple rule:\n   Instance member → current object → `this`",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "package _1inheritanceEx01;\n\npublic class Usemanager {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Manager m=new Manager();\n        m.setEmp(\"Ashish\",50000);\n        m.setBonus(50000);\n        System.out.println(\"Mannager name : \"+m.getName());\n        System.out.println(\"Income \" +m.getIncome());\n    }\n}\n\n\npackage _1inheritanceEx01;\n\nclass Manager extends Emp {\n    private double bonus;\n\n    public void setBonus(double bonus) {\n        this.bonus = bonus;\n    }\n\n    public double getBonus() {\n        return bonus;\n    }\n\n    public double getIncome() {\n        double total;\n        total = getSal() + bonus;\n        return total;\n    }\n\n}\n\n\npackage _1inheritanceEx01;\n\npublic class Emp {\n    private String name;\n    private double sal;\n\n    public void setEmp(String name, double sal) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.sal = sal;\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n\n    public double getSal() {\n        return sal;\n    }\n\n}\n\n\nthis Keyword\n============\n\n1. `this` is a reference that refers to the current object.\n\n2. Inside an instance method, if we access an instance\n   member directly without an object reference, we can\n   understand it as `this.member`.\n\nExample:\n---------\npublic String getName() {\n    return name;\n}\n\nFor understanding:\n-------------------\nreturn name;\n      ↓\nreturn this.name;\n\n\n3. Similarly:\n\ngetSal();\n   ↓\nthis.getSal();\n\n\n4. When instance variable and method parameter have the\n   same name, we use `this` to differentiate them.\n\nExample:\n---------\npublic void setEmp(String name, double sal) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.sal = sal;\n}\n\nHere:\n--> this.name → instance variable\n--> name      → method parameter\n\n--> this.sal  → instance variable\n--> sal       → method parameter\n\n\n5. Important:\n   `name` does NOT need to be literally written as\n   `this.name` in the source code.\n\n   We use `this.name` mainly to understand that `name`\n   belongs to the current object.\n\n6. Simple rule:\n   Instance member → current object → `this`",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
