      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n\n# Parent Reference and Child Reference\n\nSuppose:\n\nclass Parent {\n    int a;\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    int b;\n}\n\n\n### 1. Parent reference can refer to a Child object\n\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\n\n→ Supported because **Child is-a Parent**.\n\n→ Child object contains inherited Parent members along with its own members.\n\n\nChild object\n-------------\nParent members → a\nChild members  → b\n\n\n→ Therefore, Parent reference can access Parent's accessible members:\n\n\np.a; //ok   \n\n\n→ This is called **Upcasting**.\n\n\n### 2. Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object\n\n\nChild c = new Parent ( ) ;    // ❌\n\n\n→ Parent object does not contain Child-specific members.\n\n→ Therefore, a Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reference Type vs Object Type\n\nFor:\n\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\n\n\nReference type → Parent\nObject type    → Child\n\n\n→ **Reference type determines which members can be accessed** through the reference.\n\n→ **Actual object type determines which overridden method executes at runtime.**\n\nExample:\n\n\np.parentMethod();   // ✅\np.childMethod();    // ❌\n\n\n→ `p` can access Parent's accessible members.\n\n→ `p` cannot directly access Child-specific members.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Simple Rules\n\n\nParent reference → Child object     ✅\nChild reference  → Parent object    ❌\n\nReference type → decides ACCESSIBILITY\nObject type    → decides OVERRIDDEN METHOD at runtime\n\n\n### Remember\n\n> **Child contains the Parent part, so a Parent reference can safely refer to a Child object.**\n\n> **Parent does not contain the Child-specific part, so a Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object.**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n\n# Parent Reference and Child Reference\n\nSuppose:\n\nclass Parent {\n    int a;\n}\n\nclass Child extends Parent {\n    int b;\n}\n\n\n### 1. Parent reference can refer to a Child object\n\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\n\n→ Supported because **Child is-a Parent**.\n\n→ Child object contains inherited Parent members along with its own members.\n\n\nChild object\n-------------\nParent members → a\nChild members  → b\n\n\n→ Therefore, Parent reference can access Parent's accessible members:\n\n\np.a; //ok   \n\n\n→ This is called **Upcasting**.\n\n\n### 2. Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object\n\n\nChild c = new Parent ( ) ;    // ❌\n\n\n→ Parent object does not contain Child-specific members.\n\n→ Therefore, a Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reference Type vs Object Type\n\nFor:\n\n\nParent p = new Child();\n\n\n\nReference type → Parent\nObject type    → Child\n\n\n→ **Reference type determines which members can be accessed** through the reference.\n\n→ **Actual object type determines which overridden method executes at runtime.**\n\nExample:\n\n\np.parentMethod();   // ✅\np.childMethod();    // ❌\n\n\n→ `p` can access Parent's accessible members.\n\n→ `p` cannot directly access Child-specific members.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Simple Rules\n\n\nParent reference → Child object     ✅\nChild reference  → Parent object    ❌\n\nReference type → decides ACCESSIBILITY\nObject type    → decides OVERRIDDEN METHOD at runtime\n\n\n### Remember\n\n> **Child contains the Parent part, so a Parent reference can safely refer to a Child object.**\n\n> **Parent does not contain the Child-specific part, so a Child reference cannot directly refer to a Parent object.**\n",
